{"id": "de6b711ef08091c0:tutorial:gpt-6-luna:", "source_id": "de6b711ef08091c0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/how-stock-markets-work/public-companies", "topic": "Public Companies", "subtopic": null, "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "In general, we use the term to refer to a company that has public reporting obligations.", "question": null, "answer": "A public company, in the source’s general usage, is a company with public reporting obligations. These obligations include regularly filing reports and other materials with the SEC so shareholders can stay informed. For example, annual reports include audited annual financial statements, while quarterly reports cover financial statements and business results for the quarter and year to date.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1596, "output_tokens": 285}}
{"id": "de6b711ef08091c0:qa:gpt-6-luna:", "source_id": "de6b711ef08091c0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/how-stock-markets-work/public-companies", "topic": "Public Companies", "subtopic": null, "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "first, the company’s securities trade on public markets; and second, the company discloses certain business and financial information regularly to the public.", "question": "What are two commonly understood features of a public company?", "answer": "A company may be considered public if its securities trade on public markets and it regularly discloses certain business and financial information to the public. The source also uses “public company” to refer generally to a company with public reporting obligations.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1596, "output_tokens": 175}}
{"id": "de6b711ef08091c0:misconception:gpt-6-luna:", "source_id": "de6b711ef08091c0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/how-stock-markets-work/public-companies", "topic": "Public Companies", "subtopic": null, "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "There are instances, however, where the securities of a company that does not regularly report business and financial information to the public are nonetheless traded on smaller public markets.", "question": "Misconception: If a company’s securities trade on a public market, it must regularly report business and financial information to the public. Is that always true?", "answer": "No. The source explains that some companies’ securities trade on smaller public markets even though those companies do not regularly report business and financial information. Trading on a public market alone does not necessarily mean the company has regular public reporting obligations.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1596, "output_tokens": 312}}
